To address your concerns about challenging edges, alternative techniques include using the "Select and Mask" tool in combination with the "Refine Edge" tool to manually refine the selection around your hair and other intricate details. You can also try using a layer mask to paint over areas where the automatic detection may have missed or included too much of the background. Additionally, some photo editing software offers advanced selection tools like the "Frequency Separation" technique, which can help separate the subject from the background based on texture and color.

For the white background photo, to avoid over-smoothing or flattening features while making the lighting appear softer, you can try using a combination of the "Exposure" and "Contrast" adjustments in Adobe Lightroom or Photoshop. Start by reducing the exposure to bring down the highlights, and then adjust the contrast to add depth back into the image. You can also use the "Clarity" slider to add a bit of texture and depth to the image without over-sharpening it.

To make it easier to revisit edits later, it's a good idea to work non-destructively by using layers and adjustment layers in your photo editing software. This will allow you to go back and adjust individual edits without affecting the rest of the image. Additionally, saving your work as a PSD or TIFF file will preserve all the layers and adjustments, making it easier to revisit and modify the edits later. It's also a good practice to keep a copy of the original image and make a new copy for each iteration of edits, so you can easily compare and revert back to previous versions if needed.

