Both MRI and CT scans have their own set of risks and limitations. MRI scans are generally considered safe, but they can cause claustrophobia in some individuals and are not suitable for people with certain metal implants. The contrast agents used in MRI scans, such as gadolinium, are typically safe but can cause allergic reactions in rare cases.

CT scans, on the other hand, use X-rays and therefore expose patients to ionizing radiation, which carries a small risk of cancer. The contrast agents used in CT scans, such as iodine, can also cause allergic reactions and kidney problems in rare cases.

As for whether it makes sense to push for both scans if the primary cause isn't obvious after the first one, it's generally best to follow the recommendation of your healthcare provider. They will be able to determine the most appropriate course of action based on your symptoms, medical history, and the results of the initial scan. In some cases, a combination of scans may be necessary to get a comprehensive understanding of the underlying condition, but this should be determined on a case-by-case basis. It's essential to discuss the potential risks and benefits of each scan with your healthcare provider to make an informed decision.

