You might say, "Hey, I wanted to follow up on our previous conversations about spending more time together and discussing our future plans. I've noticed that we haven't made much progress on these topics, and I'm starting to feel a bit concerned. Can we talk about what's holding us back from moving forward on these issues? Is there something specific that's causing you to feel hesitant or unsure?"

Additionally, you could try to identify any potential areas of compromise or common ground that you can work towards together. Ask your partner about their vision for your relationship and what they hope to achieve in the short-term and long-term. Listen actively to their response and see if there are any areas where you can find mutual agreement.

It's also essential to reflect on your own feelings, needs, and deal-breakers in the relationship. Ask yourself what you are willing to work on and compromise on, and what are non-negotiables for you. Having a clear understanding of your own boundaries and expectations can help you communicate more effectively with your partner and make a more informed decision about the future of your relationship. 

By taking these proactive steps, you can gain a better understanding of whether the issues you're facing are resolvable with effort or if they are indicative of a deeper incompatibility that may require more significant changes or even a re-evaluation of the relationship.

